umns give a checked lower bound for circuits with NOT gates. The open question asks how much one can save across a graph and its complement.","rows":["L-meta-19361","L-meta-19158","L-meta-19159","L-meta-17183","L-meta-17185","L-meta-17187","L-meta-17166"]},{"id":"several-outputs-product","title":"Boolean matrix products of fixed outer size: the plain circuit is least, NOT gates counted, at every inner size","status":"support","program":"meta_complexity","why":"For a 2 by n matrix times an n by 2 matrix, with n positive, the checker excludes circuits of at most 8n - 5 gates even when NOT gates are allowed. This supplies a comparison for circuits that share work across outputs.","rows":["L-meta-17111","L-meta-17895","L-meta-17774"]},{"id":"gates-removed-one-input-at-a-time","title":"Lower bounds by fixing one named input and counting the gates that go, as a case analysis for outside provers","status":"live","program":"meta_complexity","why":"For a graph with at least three rows whose neighborhoods are pairwise incomparable and an AND/OR representation within budget, can one delete a row and save two gates, or find a circuit within that budget that reads each row exactly once in a binary OR?","rows":["L-lib-375","L-lib-376","L-meta-25481","L-meta-25489","L-meta-25491","L-lib-361","L-lib-362","L-lib-363","L-lib-364","L-lib-365","L-lib-368"]},{"id":"candidate-family","title":"The search for a family of graphs with a simple edge rule that is expensive to represent","status":"first line","program":"meta_complexity","why":"The checker connected the rule at five bits to its 32 by 32 table and accepted a circuit of at most 201 AND/OR gates for that table. The bench is testing whether circuits for versions with fewer rounds extend to the round count used by the family.","rows":["L-meta-25492","L-meta-25493","L-lib-372","L-meta-25483","L-meta-25484","L-meta-25494","L-meta-25495","L-meta-25496","L-meta-25497","L-meta-25498","L-meta-17144","L-meta-17145","L-meta-17146","L-meta-17153"]},{"id":"small-circuits-found","title":"The other direction: circuits found for small cases of hard problems, and two copies of a graph for less than twice one","status":"stopped","program":"meta_complexity","why":"Single-size searches for small circuits were stood down. The two-copy crown circuits remain examples of work shared across copies.","rows":["L-meta-17124","L-meta-18044","L-meta-18130","L-meta-17709","L-meta-8977","L-meta-18006"]},{"id":"table-representing-cost","title":"Table: the exact cost of representing each small bipartite graph by a circuit of two-input AND and OR gates","status":"stopped","program":"meta_complexity","why":"Stopped as a line on 25 Nov; the table stays as data. No table at a size we can compute can hold an entry past the linear threshold: at four vertices a side the dearest graph costs at most 11 gates against a threshold of 16 (computed).","rows":["L-meta-5421","L-lib-340"]},{"id":"table-first-not-gate","title":"Table: exact least size of two-input AND/OR circuits for the edge functions of small graphs, with and without NOT gates","status":"stopped","program":"meta_complexity","why":"Stopped as a line; the table stays as data. Every decided entry says a NOT gate saves nothing, and by our own constructions (computed, not checked) the ways we know of using a NOT gate first pay beyond every size we solve exactly, so these tables are blind to them at these sizes; that does not show that no NOT gate pays below.","rows":["L-meta-5423","L-meta-5430"]},{"id":"promise-model-method","title":"What a lower-bound method for representing a graph could be that does not eliminate gates one at a time","status":"stopped","program":"meta_complexity","why":"Stopped under this name on 25 Nov: it became the line on bounds above three gates a vertex, listed first.","rows":["L-gct-233"]},{"id":"probe-algorithm-to-bound","title":"A fresh look at turning a faster circuit-analysis algorithm into a lower bound","status":"stopped","program":"circuit_lower_bounds","why":"A one-day look by two seats at where the earlier candidate algorithms did not look; three objects tried and dropped, one narrower place named.","rows":["L-clb-024"]},{"id":"probe-other-direction","title":"Where a fast algorithm could hide that the earlier eliminations did not cover","status":"stopped","program":"p_eq_np_search","why":"A one-day look by two seats; one concrete kind, cancellation built from constant-size gadgets, was run on a small family and eliminated at the sizes tried.","rows":[]},{"id":"conjecture-sweep","title":"Conjectures stated in the complexity literature, each begun as one exact table of small cases","status":"dormant","program":"meta_complexity","why":"The sweep is parked. Its tables remain available as tests of future conjectures.","rows":[]},{"id":"clean-ands","title":"Conjecture: on graphs with no 4-cycle, some least AND/OR circuit of two-input gates has only clean AND gates","status":"stopped","program":"meta_complexity","why":"The checker accepted a graph of sides at most six on which allowing an AND gate to light a missing edge can reduce the number of gates. This ends the proposed simplification for every graph.","rows":["L-meta-25474","L-meta-3754","L-meta-3760","L-meta-1407","L-meta-5416","L-meta-5414","L-meta-5415","L-meta-5391","L-meta-5248","L-meta-5280","L-meta-5383","L-meta-5385","L-meta-5388","L-meta-5401","L-meta-5403","L-meta-5399","L-meta-5332","L-meta-5387","L-meta-5012","L-meta-5126","L-meta-5138","L-meta-5412"]},{"id":"double-reread","title":"A price for incidences that a circuit re-reads at both the point and the line","status":"support","program":"meta_complexity","why":"A checked inequality counts repeated reads on planes. The proposed count joining it to a lower bound failed a small test in the earlier search.","rows":["L-meta-2672","L-meta-3558","L-meta-3565"]},{"id":"read-2","title":"AND/OR circuits where every incident pair has a proof reading its point and its line at most twice: a size bound","status":"stopped","program":"meta_complexity","why":"Stopped: checked, from an outside proof: on a plane of order q with N points such circuits of two-input gates have at least N q / 100 gates. The line moved on.","rows":["L-meta-1942"]},{"id":"one-output","title":"Conjecture: with two-input AND/OR gates and no NOT gate, a plane's incidence function needs more than linear size","status":"stopped","program":"meta_complexity","why":"The main target of this line, a statement about circuits with no NOT gate; a bound here does not by itself say anything about circuits with NOT gates.","rows":["L-meta-1629"]},{"id":"one-side-once","title":"AND/OR circuits of two-input gates where every incident pair is reached reading its line once or its point once","status":"stopped","program":"meta_complexity","why":"A rung between the checked case and the first line, argued on paper from a checked count for sums of sets; its own row is not filed yet, it waits on one definition.","rows":["L-meta-529"]},{"id":"few-cross-gates","title":"AND/OR circuits of two-input gates with few AND gates whose two inputs both depend on lines","status":"support","program":"meta_complexity","why":"Checked counts for a restricted class and one conjecture that joins them; the count loses a factor for each such gate, which is the wall the first line must pass.","rows":["L-meta-1622","L-meta-1623","L-meta-1624","L-meta-1625","L-meta-1690","L-meta-1712"]},{"id":"heavy-inputs","title":"Rejecting the largest false inputs: what a circuit must do on inputs with many points and lines on","status":"stopped","program":"meta_complexity","why":"Every cheap circuit on file accepts these inputs (by script, not checked); the question is what rejecting a fraction of them costs.","rows":["L-meta-1693","L-meta-1751","L-meta-1749","L-meta-1692"]},{"id":"graph-form","title":"Conjecture: on graphs with no short cycle, AND/OR circuits of two-input gates need gates linear in the number of edges","status":"stopped","program":"meta_complexity","why":"The graph formulation remains in the record. The current work on graph representation is tracked under the explicit family and the row-deletion question.","rows":["L-meta-606","L-meta-612","L-meta-1733","L-meta-1735","L-meta-1708","L-meta-1710"]},{"id":"petersen","title":"The exact circuit size of the Petersen graph's edge function when NOT gates are allowed","status":"support","program":"meta_complexity","why":"The Petersen graph remains a test case for checking what a deletion can cost in a graph of degree three.","rows":["L-meta-651","L-meta-670","L-meta-1665","L-meta-1666","L-meta-1673","L-meta-1674","L-meta-1675","L-meta-1676","L-meta-1677","L-meta-1678","L-meta-1697","L-meta-1743","L-meta-1745"]},{"id":"degree-three","title":"Conjecture: graphs of maximum degree 3 with no short cycle need edges plus cover minus one gates, NOT gates allowed","status":"stopped","program":"meta_complexity","why":"The cubic case needs a new step at a vertex of degree three; the leaf step is checked.","rows":["L-meta-519","L-meta-1592","L-meta-1593"]},{"id":"atlas","title":"The atlas: exact least circuit sizes of small graphs and small pieces of planes","status":"support","program":"meta_complexity","why":"Data to read before guessing a law; lower bounds are checked only up to a measured size, the rest is listed as computed.","rows":["L-meta-982","L-meta-984","L-meta-927"]},{"id":"vocabulary","title":"New measures of a circuit, each a definition with sanity lemmas and its values on the atlas","status":"support","program":"meta_complexity","why":"A measure is kept only if it predicts sizes on the atlas where gate counts and covers do not.","rows":["L-meta-1784","L-meta-1785","L-meta-1786"]},{"id":"one-not-several-outputs","title":"One NOT gate on the line functions of a plane (several outputs)","status":"stopped","program":"meta_complexity","why":"Stopped: its end statement is of a kind already known for several outputs, so it does not move the one-output question.","rows":["L-meta-952","L-meta-1389"]},{"id":"few-not-ladder","title":"Circuits with few NOT gates computing several outputs","status":"stopped","program":"meta_complexity","why":"Stopped: checked for every plane given as data, and a statement of this kind for several outputs is already known.","rows":["L-meta-1397"]},{"id":"line-degree","title":"AND/OR circuits of two-input gates of bounded line-degree","status":"stopped","program":"meta_complexity","why":"Stopped: line-degree three is checked, and climbing one degree at a time only changes constants.","rows":["L-meta-1390","L-meta-1393"]},{"id":"arc-leak","title":"AND/OR lists of two-input gates whose line tests are right on arcs","status":"stopped","program":"meta_complexity","why":"Stopped: its end statement prices only a circuit shape already covered, and every per-line count tried on it failed on an explicit circuit (by script, not checked).","rows":["L-meta-1536","L-meta-1534","L-meta-1535"]},{"id":"depth-probes","title":"Monotone formula depth of small graphs and gadgets","status":"stopped","program":"meta_complexity","why":"Stopped: every probe came back on the expected side, so the space holds no surprise at these sizes.","rows":["L-meta-1398","L-meta-1400","L-meta-1402","L-meta-1507","L-meta-1620"]},{"id":"level-spaces","title":"Permanent against determinant: linear spaces on which the permanent stays constant","status":"dormant","program":"gct","why":"No seat is on it; two small probes remain on the desk.","rows":["L-gct-086","L-gct-229","L-gct-231"]},{"id":"proof-complexity","title":"Proof complexity: lower bounds for proof systems","status":"support","program":"proof_complexity","why":"The proof-complexity bench is testing how a restriction on edges affects the line count of a refutation. A general simulation would let it compare sparse and complete pigeonhole formulas.","rows":["L-pcx-203","L-pcx-204"]},{"id":"algorithm-search","title":"The search for a fast algorithm for satisfiability","status":"dormant","program":"p_eq_np_search","why":"The equal-side bench is comparing ways to evaluate the candidate rule as its round count grows. That circuit question is tracked under the candidate family.","rows":["L-alg-002","L-alg-003","L-alg-004"]},{"id":"locality-gap","title":"Local reductions and the minimum circuit size problem","status":"dormant","program":"meta_complexity","why":"Parked: its checked rows restate a known idea on our definitions.","rows":["L-meta-043"]}],"attack_source":{"file":"plan/attack.json","commit":"ad5d477eca074d011f7b1e95ef4d6462fbdf572a","updated_at":"2026-11-27T10:02:58Z","read_by":"seat-redteam-1"},"outside_results":0}